\begin_layout Standard
Recall that subjectively,
 all we ever really know is ourselves,
 and objectively,
 everything happens in our cranium.
 So when your thoughts turn to someone,
 it is not the actual physical person that your thoughts turn to,
 but to the mental model of that person that you hold inside your head.
 You interact with the person that you think they are,
 with this mental model in your head.
 This holds not only when the person is out of sight,
 but also when you are in the same room.
 The main difference is that your model is more temporally accurate when you're in the same room:
 you can see if they are scowling or smiling.
 Which your mirror neurons interpret,
 as appropriate.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
You build this mental model out of "information" flowing through your senses.
 For text (facebook posts without photos,
 or this email) the only impression you can build is from the communicated words.
 Add vision,
 and there is so much more.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Before connection happens,
 there are first impressions.
 Most people work hard to shape those impressions:
 hair–cut,
 clothing,
 makeup,
 body language.
 As connections develop,
 from fleeting and momentary to acquaintanceship to friendship,
 the visual aspect falls away,
 because it contributes less and less to "who this person is" in your mental model of them.
 For example,
 you might meet someone and think "wow,
 they're ugly",
 but as you get to know them,
 you say "they're beautiful inside".
 In literature,
 this is the Beauty and the Beast trope.
 Sadly,
 the converse seems more commonly encountered:
 the beautiful–looking person who proves toxic to be around.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
"Who this person really is" is your model of their spirit.
 I choose the word "spirit" instead of "personality" because "personality" is clinical,
 psychological,
 analytical,
 cold,
 under a bright white light on a shining steel table.
 The word "spirit" is the root of "spiritual",
 which promptly flies off into the woo of mysticism and religion.
 It claims that there is a magic living being in there.
 (which there is.) When one craves "connection",
 one wants a (reciprocal) relationship with that spirit.
 The connection is subjectively felt.
 Intuitively,
 not really explainable with words,
 although millions of novels strike chords and resonate.
 Pride and Prejudice is a paragon of using text,
 words,
 the same text/words we use on the internet,
 to generate emotional resonances in your amygdala or whatever neural structure in your brain that it is,
 that "resonates" when you attain "connection".
 (Mirror neurons,
 from what I can tell,
 are meant to apply to limb movement,
 of modeling the intent of movement.
 Emotional connection is more than that.)
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
In–person gatherings are democratizing.
 You may have spent hours beforehand,
 working on clothing and makeup,
 but once you "go live" at the meeting or the party,
 we're all equal,
 sink or swim minute by minute.
 You're dancing or performing or engaging or connecting or whatever,
 in real time.
 Your communicative talents are primary,
 here.
 Corporate executives get training in how to be socially effective.
 It's a skill,
 a talent;
 practice it and get good,
 don't and you won't.
 In–person meetings are great,
 except for those who are socially awkward.
 Perhaps the whimsy of eating an apple defuses,
 disarms prior expectations as to "what you should be like" (what you should act like) when you meet other people face–to–face.
 You can be a socially awkward clod,
 but as long as you can bite into an apple and not collapse into a quivering heap of jello,
 you'll be OK.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Social media posts are just ...
 different.
 Narcissists can spend vast hours arranging the look that they want for a photo or a short reel.
 They never have to "go live",
 they can rehearse and rehearse and make great efforts to craft an image.
 That image is much like the image from face–to–face first impressions.
 Unlike face–to–face,
 though,
 it becomes a bit harder to move past first impressions.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
"Connection" can also be a one–way street.
 An entire generation of teenagers developed a connection to Brad Pitt.
 He lives on in their souls.
 Or rather,
 the mental model of him lives on in their craniums,
 until the day they die (or suffer a brain injury that erases this memory).
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The point here is that "media" are communications channels,
 and the "connection" you build when using text+video is no less authentic than in–person.
 It's just different.
 Well,
 but for one thing:
 I can't hug or kiss over the internet.
 The lovelorn pining away for hugs that morph into sex aren't gonna get that over the internet.
 (yet?
 according to the futurists who write science fiction.)
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The other point here is that,
 for subjective reality,
 and it's objective presence in the confines of my cranium,
 there is always "media".
 When I'm in–person,
 the subjective me,
 dassein,
 is walled off behind optic nerves and auditory canals.
 I still cannot get out from behind those.
 We do not yet have mind–meld technology,
 and if and when that makes it's appearance,
 well,
 a whole new conception of connection will develop.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
So I think that ultimately,
 we are always just spirits,
 and always more or less alone because of that.
 The psychological issue is that we all have mammalian brains,
 with assorted neural wiring that expresses a vast spectrum of (subjective) emotional states,
 and that "connection" is central to many of them.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
This essay is too long already,
 so I'll stop here,
 but I suspect that the need for "connection" goes far,
 far deeper.
 I subscribe to the idea of "basal cognition",
 viz that prokaryotes and eukaryotes are (also) conscious (this is hotly discussed these days;
 Micheal Levin,
 among others,
 writes reasonably lucidly on that) and I suspect that "connection" is meaningful and desired there,
 as well (viz.
 bacterial quorum sensing,
 or the slime–mold two–armed bandit.) So this spiritual desire for connection happens not just at the human level,
 but appears to be a fundamental property of interacting agentic systems at all size scales.
 I won't call it "a property of the universe" because that is too woo.
 But the universe does manifest interacting agentic systems (at all size scales),
 so connection is one off from that.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
-- Linas.
